    "lawCode": "Amd §§1113 & 3230, rpld §3230 sub§ (g), Ins L",
    "lawSection": "Insurance Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S2114B\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the insurance law, in relation to\naccelerated life insurance payments for certain chronic conditions;\nand repealing subsection (g) of section 3230 of the insurance law\nrelating thereto\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nTo provide refined chronic illness triggers for an accelerated death\nbenefit by conforming New York law to the Internal Revenue Code.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1 of the bill would amend section 1113(a)(1) (D) of the\ninsurance law to refine the long term care triggers for qualification\nfor an accelerated death benefit under a life insurance policy by\nremoving the reference to the issuer being a qualified long term care\ninsurance carrier under Section 4980c of the Internal Revenue Code\n\nSection 2 of the bill would amend section 3230 (f) and (g) of the\ninsurance law to conform with the change made to insurance law section\n1113(a)(1)(D) in section 1 of the bill in reference to removing the\nlanguage that the issuer be a qualified long term care insurer\n\nSection 3 is the effective date.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nThis proposal would amend § 1 113(a)(1) (D) of the insurance law to\nrefine the long term care trigger for qualification for an accelerated\ndeath benefit under a life insurance policy. The current standard for\ntriggering payment of an accelerated death benefit for chronic illness\n(i.e., in need of long term care services) under a life insurance\npolicy offered by a life insurer that is not also a long term care\ninsurer is more restrictive than the Internal Revenue Code (IRC §\n101(g)), as well as other state laws, which require the insured to be\ncertified as having a chronic illness, but do not require permanent\nconfinement, as current New York law does. This proposal will enable\nlife insurers who are not also long term care insurers to offer an\naccelerated death benefit rider with a non-permanent confinement\ntrigger in New York.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nNew bill.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nImmediately.\n",
